@model Homespool.Host.Pages.PrinterFormFactor
@*
    A printer, drawn here rather than fetched from anywhere.

    OURS, and it has to be. Homespool talks to other people's machines and is an independent
    alternative to the software that ships with them - visual-identity.md §2 records the house blue
    being chosen as "not Prusa's orange" on exactly that reasoning. A recognisable per-model
    silhouette would be somebody else's product design with our stroke width on it, and a photograph
    would be worse. So: two generic shapes, an open frame and a closed box, both of which a dozen
    manufacturers build.

    The 96 viewBox and stroke-width 4 are the mark's own geometry (_BrandMark.cshtml), so these sit
    beside it as the same family of drawing rather than as clip art that wandered in.

    NO ids, defs or masks anywhere in here, unlike the mark. This renders once per tile and ids are
    document-global once inlined - the mark's comment records that trap, and six tiles on a page is
    precisely where it would bite. Occlusion is done by not overlapping anything instead.

    aria-hidden, because the plaque underneath already names the printer and says what it is doing.
    The drawing tells a sighted reader which tile to look at; it carries no fact of its own, and
    labelling it would make a screen reader announce "printer" before every name.
*@
@if (Model == Homespool.Host.Pages.PrinterFormFactor.Enclosed)
{
    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 96 96"
         fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="4"
         stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
         aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" class="printer-icon printer-icon-enclosed">
        @* The box is the whole distinction, so it is the outermost and heaviest shape. *@
        <rect x="12" y="14" width="72" height="72" rx="6" />
        @* A lid seam, which is what stops the box reading as a plain crate. *@
        <path d="M12 30 H84" />
        @* Gantry carriage and nozzle, hanging where a person expects them. *@
        <rect x="39" y="40" width="18" height="11" rx="3" />
        <path d="M48 51 V57" />
        @* The bed, short of the walls so it reads as inside the box rather than as a shelf - the
           opposite of the open cut below, where the bed deliberately runs out past the frame. *@
        <path d="M26 70 H64" />
        @* The door seam, full height from the lid down. A floating handle stub was tried here first
           and read as a stray tick beside the carriage at tile size; a seam that meets both edges is
           unambiguous, and it is what says this box is a thing you open rather than a crate. *@
        <path d="M70 30 V86" />
    </svg>
}
else
{
    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 96 96"
         fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="4"
         stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
         aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" class="printer-icon printer-icon-open">
        @* A portal frame: two uprights and a beam, and deliberately NO floor. An earlier cut had a
           base bar, which closed the silhouette into a rectangle and made this all but identical to
           the box above at tile size - the distinction the two drawings exist for, lost. *@
        <path d="M24 80 V26" />
        <path d="M72 80 V26" />
        <path d="M20 26 H76" />
        @* Carriage on the beam, nozzle under it. *@
        <rect x="39" y="32" width="18" height="11" rx="3" />
        <path d="M48 43 V49" />
        @* The bed, running WIDER than the frame and crossing both uprights. It is the cue that reads
           as "open" at a glance - a bed that slides out through the sides cannot be in a box - and it
           borrows the trick the house mark already uses, where the spool is too big for the house and
           crosses its floor (visual-identity.md §3). *@
        <path d="M14 66 H82" />
    </svg>
}
